<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    Staff rehearse realistic guest interactions on their own time, instead
    of learning live in front of a paying table. Every rep gets immediate
    feedback — not a script to memorize, but real practice at handling
    whatever a guest actually says.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>What staff train on</h2>
  <ul class="marketing-feature-list">
    <li><strong>Menu</strong> — know the food and drink list cold, not just skim it once.</li>
    <li><strong>Off-Menu knowledge</strong> — answer the questions that aren't printed anywhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Recommendations &amp; Pairings</strong> — guide a table with confidence, not guesswork.</li>
    <li><strong>Handling Complaints</strong> — stay composed and on-brand when a table pushes back.</li>
    <li><strong>Upselling</strong> — offer more without it feeling like a pitch.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>Why Verbal Descriptions Matter</h2>
  <ul class="marketing-feature-list">
    <li><strong>Justifies the Premium Price</strong> — Fine dining often involves incredibly labor-intensive, multi-day culinary processes (e.g., a 48-hour bone marrow reduction, lacto-fermentation, or specialized curing). If a server simply drops a plate and says, "Here is your steak," the guest cannot appreciate the immense skill and time that justifies a $150 price tag.</li>
    <li><strong>Enhances the Flavor Perception</strong> — Knowing how a dish was prepared primes the diner's palate. Explaining that a component was "cold-smoked over cherrywood" or "compressed with elderflower" tells the guest exactly what subtle notes to look for, making the tasting experience deeper and more mindful.</li>
    <li><strong>Builds Trust and Safety</strong> — True fine dining requires flawless navigation of allergies and dietary restrictions. A server who can confidently detail every emulsification, oil base, and cooking medium reassures guests that they are in expert, safe hands.</li>
    <li><strong>Creates the Narrative Arc</strong> — Great meals tell a story. The server acts as the narrator, bridging the gap between the kitchen's hidden creative process and the guest's table. They contextualize the chef's vision, regional inspirations, and technical mastery.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>The Anatomy of a Fine Dining Explanation</h2>
  <p>
    Professional service staff are trained to keep descriptions concise but
    evocative, typically hitting three key pillars in under 30 seconds:
  </p>
  <ul class="marketing-feature-list">
    <li><strong>The Method</strong> — Highlighting the specific technique used (e.g., pan-seared, sous-vide, confited).</li>
    <li><strong>The Provenance</strong> — Mentioning the premium or hyper-local source of the main ingredient (e.g., line-caught Atlantic cod, A5 Miyazaki wagyu).</li>
    <li><strong>The Finish</strong> — Explaining how the dish is brought together at the end, such as a tableside pour of a consommé or the shaving of fresh truffles.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="marketing-section">
  <h2>Why it matters for your restaurant</h2>
  <p>
    Consistent training means consistent service, shift after shift, no
    matter who's on the floor. Staff walk into a shift already prepared —
    without pulling anyone off the floor to learn — and that readiness
    shows up directly in the guest experience and in who comes back.
  </p>
</section>

